  <h2>How was this page compiled?</h2>

  <p>It is a compilation, not a survey. Nobody was polled, nothing was modeled, and no figure here originates with hubStudio. Every band was read off the surface that publishes it, and the rule for getting onto the page was fixed before the reading started.</p>

  <p>A figure qualifies only if it clears four conditions at once. It has to be an absolute amount, in the currency its own source publishes, against a unit that source states. It has to have been read directly on the publishing surface: a seller's own pricing page, a national statistical release, or a trade body's own instrument. It has to carry a date. And the way it was produced has to be statable in one sentence.</p>

  <p>That last condition does most of the work. It is why a rate card counts as evidence of what one seller publicly charges on the day it was read, and never as a market average. Where two or more independent cards agree, the band is a range. Where one card carries the figure alone, the table says single-source, because one card is not a market.</p>

  <p>The collection window: pricing pages were read on 9 and 10 September 2026 and every load-bearing one was re-read on 17 September 2026. Three Chinese rate cards carry their own dates, from June 2023 to July 2026. The statistical series carry their own reference periods, which are not the same as the date they were read. Trade body instruments date from January 2023, 2026 and March 2025.</p>

  <p>Here is what was thrown out, and why.</p>

