  <p>That last row is not a technicality and it will not be softened. What can be offered instead of independence: no hubStudio figure appears anywhere on this page, every number traces to a surface hubStudio does not control, and everything discarded is listed above rather than quietly dropped.</p>

  <h2>What happened when these figures were re-checked a week later?</h2>

  <p>Two of them moved. This is the part worth reading before you use any cost band from anywhere, including this one.</p>

  <p>Every source here was read at the start of September and re-fetched on 17 September 2026, seven days later. One studio pricing page had rebuilt how it expresses price. At the first reading it published complexity uplifts as dollar figures: $75 for ghost mannequin or oversized work, $100 for jewelry and watches, $150 for a hand model image, $190 for a full-body model image. Seven days on, not one of those four numbers appears anywhere on the card. The same ladder is now published as credit multipliers, one credit for a standard image rising to four for a full-body model, against headline rates of "from $50 per image" and "from $95 per image".</p>

  <p>Those four figures are therefore cut from this page. They could be reconstructed by multiplying the multipliers against the $50 floor, and three of the four old numbers would reappear, which is exactly why it is not done here: "from $50" is a floor, not a rate, and the arithmetic would publish a price the card does not.</p>

  <p>The second change was quieter. The national employer compensation series advanced a reference period, and the newer release moved the benefits load from 1.4294 to 1.4287. Both round to 1.43, so nothing derived from it on this page shifts by a printed digit. It is recorded anyway, because a figure that was checked and a figure that was remembered look identical on the page, right up to the moment one of them is wrong.</p>

  <p>The practical rule: treat any cost band published without a date as a historical artifact, because in this category it usually is one.</p>

  <h2>What does a product or model image cost?</h2>

  <p>Every band below is quoted against the unit its own source states, and where two sources use different units they get two rows, never one merged range. That rule holds for the rest of this page.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put standard ecommerce capture between roughly $39 and $50 an image. One card prices a photo at $39 and adds a $149 studio fee per booking; the other starts product work at $50 an image against a $300 project minimum.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published product photography pricing pages in this category, two independent cards read directly on 17 September 2026, prices quoted against the unit each card states.</p>

  <p>Model work is priced on one card only, so it is labeled single-source in the table and here.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put a standard product image at $39 to $50, a model or lifestyle image at about $95, a short video clip at about $93 and a single UGC video at about $89, with editing add-ons around $49 each and a finished pro edit at about $199.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly, prices quoted as each card states them.</p>

  <p>Read that one carefully. Only the $39 to $50 figure sits on two cards. Every other number in it comes from a single card, which is why the master table labels each of them single-source rather than letting them inherit the triangulated label from the row above. Quoted whole, without that caveat, the band reads far more solid than the evidence under it.</p>

  <p>The Chinese side of the same work prices differently and publishes more of its conditions.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese commercial photography studio's published 2026 rate card lists flat-lay shots at 80 to 150 RMB an image, in-studio model shots at 200 to 300 RMB, retouching at 50 to 200 RMB an image and short video at 3,000 to 15,000 RMB a piece, on a five-style minimum.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published Chinese ecommerce photography rate card, dated 20 July 2026, described by its publisher as its own executing rates for the Guangzhou and Pearl River Delta region. One studio's book, no survey behind it.</p>

  <p>No conversion is printed between those two sets of figures, deliberately. Applying one day's exchange rate to one regional card would imply a precision the card does not have, and the minimums and inclusions differ on both sides. Read each in its own currency. <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">The ecommerce design service pages</a> carry the same discipline for the deliverables themselves.</p>

  <h2>What does retouch cost?</h2>

  <p>Four different services share this one word, and the published prices sit more than a hundredfold apart because of it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in this category put the floor for ecommerce image editing between roughly $0.25 and $1.20 an image, with one card adding a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month on top.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly and compared, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>On the 17 September re-read the top of that band reads $1.19, for image masking and multi-clipping, which is the figure the master table carries. Two specialty operations sit above it on the same card, at $2.99 and $3.99, and they were outside the band at both readings.</p>

  <p>Against capture, that is almost nothing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Against published capture of $39 to $50 an image, a per-image retouch price of about $0.95 is between 1.9 and 2.4 percent of the capture line, and the full published retouch band runs from about 0.5 to about 3.1 percent of it. Retouch is not production.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two published pricing categories, both collected September 2026, divided one into the other. Capture from two studio cards, retouch from two editing cards, all four read directly.</p>

  <p>The access conditions matter more than the unit price at low volume.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One published retouch card guarantees 200 edited images a day with next-morning delivery, and declines anyone under 1,000 images a year, sending them to a trial instead. Its $95 monthly platform fee doubles the unit price at 100 images a month and adds about 10 percent at 1,000.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published image editing pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026. The access condition and the fee are stated on the card, the amortization is division.</p>

  <h2>What does a detail page cost?</h2>

  <p>This is the row where the unit problem stops being theoretical.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published Chinese rate cards for ecommerce detail-page design disagree by roughly an order of magnitude, from 200 to 300 RMB a page at the low end of a 2023 page to 1,500 to 5,000 RMB a set on a 2026 page, where a set is defined as twenty pieces: main images, the detail-page cover, scene images and detail shots.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published Chinese design rate cards, dated 12 June 2023 and 30 April 2026, both re-read 17 September 2026, no company named. Published prices only, and the conflict is stated rather than averaged.</p>

  <p>Now divide. Fifteen hundred to five thousand RMB across twenty pieces is 75 to 250 RMB an asset, which overlaps the older page's 200 to 300 RMB almost completely. Most of that order-of-magnitude gap was never a price difference at all. It was a unit difference, and it survived three years of being quoted because neither page made the reader check.</p>

  <p>Two limits travel with that arithmetic. The 2023 figure prices one detail page while the 2026 set bundles main images, a cover and scene shots alongside detail shots, so the two still are not the same thing. And the 2026 page is sponsored content. That is precisely why it is published as a conflict rather than as a reading of the market, and why the far lower figure the same page quotes for its own sponsor's tool appears nowhere here.</p>

  <p>For the English-speaking markets there is no band at all. Searched in September 2026, every result was a listicle, an agency roundup or a tool vendor comparison, and not one price was read on a seller's own card. The absence is the finding. <a href="/resources/insights/cost-to-localize-a-campaign-for-china">What it costs to localize a campaign for China</a> covers the neighboring ground, and <a href="/solutions/platforms/tmall">the Tmall platform page</a> describes what the deliverable has to be before anyone can price it.</p>

  <h2>What does short video or a brand film cost?</h2>

  <p>Three rows, three different things.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same Chinese studio card prices a short video of 15 to 60 seconds at 3,000 to 15,000 RMB a piece, and adds a travel charge of 200 to 500 RMB for shoots outside Guangzhou.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published Chinese ecommerce photography rate card dated 20 July 2026, re-read in Chinese on 17 September 2026, the publisher's own rates for one region.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One published adaptation rate card in this category starts at 1,350 euro excluding VAT for a 15-second commercial adaptation, and states exactly what that buys: a new voice-over, new titles, a new packshot, an audio mix and mastering.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published commercial adaptation rate card, read 10 September 2026 and re-read 17 September 2026. One card, one duration, one inclusion list, and no publication date printed on the page.</p>

  <p>A clip out of a studio, a finished short, and a re-version of a film that already exists. Nobody should be adding those together.</p>

  <p>For originating a brand film there is no current figure to give you, and the reason is a small piece of category history worth knowing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US advertising agency trade body ran an annual survey of TV commercial production costs for 25 years and dropped it in February 2014. Its last data year was 2011, when a 30-second spot averaged $354,000, and the reason given was that almost nobody shoots a single 30-second commercial on its own anymore.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: trade press report of the trade body's decision, February 2014. A historical figure from a discontinued survey, not a 2026 price.</p>

  <p>Two figures circulating on this query in 2026 cite that survey's "2025 edition" and a trade body guideline's "average bid". Neither exists on any document that could be read. They are not printed here, and the reason they are worth mentioning at all is that they sit on a page ranking for exactly the question you typed.</p>

  <h2>What does generated video cost per second?</h2>

  <p>Generation is the one line in this whole page priced by the second.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published list prices put a second of generated video between roughly $0.05 and $0.60, depending on model tier and resolution, with audio included at the default on one of the two surfaces.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published generation price surfaces in this category, read directly on 10 September 2026, one credit card and one API price list, quoted per second of generated video, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>That looks decisive. It stops looking decisive once you count what gets thrown away.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Thirty seconds of finished screen time, generated once with nothing retried, costs between about $1.50 and $18.00 in generation at published list rates.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on published generation prices in this category, 10 September 2026, shown step by step on this page.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Take the same 30 seconds at four generations for each usable one and the generation bill runs about $6 to $72. Take it at the roughly 300 to 1 ratio one documented production disclosed and the same 30 seconds runs about $450 to $5,400.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on published generation prices in this category, 10 September 2026, applied to the two retry ratios that have been published anywhere, both shown with their limits on this page.</p>

  <p>Seventy-five times, between the two ratios anybody has published. <a href="/resources/insights/all-in-cost-of-ai-video">The all-in cost of AI video</a> works through that arithmetic in full.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No independent benchmark of generations per usable shot exists. The only rate published by anyone selling generation tools comes from that seller's own unnamed internal case work, and the only figure from a named practitioner covers a single 2024 production. The two differ by roughly seventy-five times.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: five buyer queries run in English and Chinese on 10 September 2026, top ten results assessed for each. Four of the top eight on the direct question measure detection of generated video, which is a different quantity on a different population.</p>

  <h2>What does translation or transcreation cost?</h2>

  <p>Words are the one part of this list with a settled unit and an unsettled method.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in the translation category put professional English to Simplified Chinese at roughly $0.08 to $0.18 per source word, and price transcreation per hour or per project instead, because source word count does not track the creative work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages collected across the category, September 2026, no company named. Rate cards only, no survey and no method published behind the band.</p>

  <p>Transcreation has no absolute figure anywhere, in any currency, from any publisher. The national wage series is the nearest honest proxy, and it prices a person's hour rather than a deliverable.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the United States the median interpreter or translator earned $60,170 a year, or $28.93 an hour, in May 2025, with the lowest tenth under $37,070 and the highest tenth over $103,660. Loaded at the published private-industry multiplier of about 1.43, the median hour costs an employer about $41.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period, a national establishment survey that excludes the self-employed, with the load from the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026. US data.</p>

  <h2>What does subscription access cost?</h2>

  <p>What a subscription prices is capacity, not assets, and the cards say so once you read the tier labels rather than the headline.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Unlimited-request design subscriptions in this category publish tiers between roughly $700 and $4,500 a month, billed month to month.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: category rate cards collected September 2026, published pricing pages read directly, no vendor named.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Unlimited requests" is published alongside a cap on how many requests can be worked at once. Across four published cards read in September 2026, that cap runs from one to five active items at a time, rising with the tier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, tier labels read directly off the cards, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Both cards re-read on 17 September 2026 still publish that cap in the tier label itself, in wording like "two active workstreams" and "one daily output". Nobody is hiding it. It is simply not in the headline.</p>

  <p>Above that sits the managed end, where the unit becomes an hour.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Managed design subscriptions in this category publish monthly floors between roughly $6,000 and $15,000, with dedicated tiers from roughly $30,000 a month on a twelve-month commitment.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: category rate cards collected September 2026, published pricing pages read directly, no vendor named.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Divide the published managed tiers by their published hours and the category prices access at almost exactly $50 a creative hour: $5,995 for 120 hours and $8,995 for 180 hours.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on one published rate card in this category, collected September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Those hours expire monthly. And the commitment, once you carry the published terms out to a year, is the largest single figure on this page.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">At the dedicated end the same card starts at $30,000 a month plus the $1,000 monthly software fee, on a twelve-month term, which commits the buyer to $372,000 for the year before a single asset is briefed.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on a published rate card in this category, read 10 September 2026 and re-read 17 September 2026, published floor plus published fee times twelve months, no vendor named.</p>

  <p><a href="/resources/insights/subscription-or-managed-production">Subscription or managed production</a> takes the unit-of-purchase question apart properly.</p>

  <h2>What does an in-house team cost?</h2>

  <p>The only sources in this entire compilation that pass all four tests are the national wage series. They price an hour of a person, never an asset.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Median annual wages in the national occupational wage series for May 2025 run $62,960 for graphic designers, $75,420 for film and video editors, $74,990 for camera operators and $114,850 for art directors.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period, full establishment survey.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">US photographers earned a median $44,660 a year, $21.47 an hour, in May 2025, with the lowest tenth under $14.82 an hour and the highest tenth over $44.14. At the published private-industry load of about 1.43, the median hour costs an employer about $31.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period, a national establishment survey, with the load from the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026. US data.</p>

  <p>Salary is not the cost, which is the mistake that sinks most in-house models before they start.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Turn that into the multiplier a budget holder actually needs and salary understates the real cost by about 43 percent: $46.60 divided by $32.60 is 1.43.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026 reference period, shown on the page.</p>

  <p>That series has since published a June 2026 reference period, released on 9 September 2026, which puts the same load at 1.43. Nothing below moves.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Four US seats at national median pay, an art director, a film editor and two graphic designers, come to $316,190 in salary and about $452,000 a year once the published benefits load is added. Spread across 8,320 paid hours, that is $54.32 an hour, before recruitment, idle time between campaigns, software or space, none of which has a published benchmark.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on the national occupational wage series, May 2025, and the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026, US data, with a 2,080-hour year stated as a convention.</p>

  <p>Outside the US the figures exist but the loading does not, so these run unloaded.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the UK the median graphic and multimedia designer job paid £31,236 a year, £32,510 for full-time jobs; photographers and audio-visual operators £30,396 and £32,643; arts officers, producers and directors £39,643 and £45,534. The median across all employee jobs was £32,890, and £39,039 full-time.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the UK Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings, 2025 provisional estimates for the tax year ending 5 April 2025, released October 2025, a 1 percent sample of payroll jobs weighted to population totals, self-employed excluded.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In China, average annual pay in culture, sport and entertainment was 126,040 yuan in non-private urban units and 61,669 yuan in private units in 2024.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: China's National Bureau of Statistics, 2024 average wages of employed persons in urban units, published 16 May 2025. An industry average from a national statistical release, not an occupation median.</p>

  <p><a href="/resources/insights/in-house-studio-vs-outsourced-production">In-house studio or outsourced production</a> builds the full loaded model from those rows.</p>

  <h2>Why do content cost benchmarks disagree with each other?</h2>

  <p>Because almost nobody publishing one is a disinterested party, and almost nobody defines the unit before pricing it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across four phrasings a buyer would type about brand content cost, 28 of the 34 results were published by a party selling the work, tool or talent being priced, and none by a statistical agency or a trade body. None of the six pages read in full stated how its own figures were collected.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search-results audit run 10 September 2026, publisher type recorded for every result and six pages read in full, no domain named.</p>

  <p>Under that, the same handful of figures recirculates from page to page with the instrument stripped off. Once a number has been relayed three times it reads like consensus, and it is the same unsourced number wearing three coats.</p>

  <p>Here are the conflicts this page carries rather than resolves, because resolving them would mean inventing something.</p>

