  <h2>What does no published band include?</h2>

  <p>The things that reliably blow up a production budget are absent from every card read for this page, across two markets.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No card read for this piece prices sample logistics, reshoots or approval rounds. Eight published price surfaces across two markets, and getting the product to the studio, getting it back, and shooting it again after a failed approval appear on none of them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, two markets, read directly in September 2026. Absence confirmed by reading each card in full.</p>

  <p>Rights are the other silence, and it runs all the way to the top of the category.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US commercial production trade body's standard bid form itemizes a production across sections A through P, from prep crew to breakout talent, and carries no line at all for media usage payments.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own free bid form, updated January 2023, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>So the instrument the category built to cost a production in full carries no line for the payments that running the work then triggers.</p>

  <p>Where the better data might have been, there are locked doors and missing columns.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The UK advertiser trade body's 2025 creative and media agency hourly rate benchmarks are released only to members who contribute their own rates, in aggregated form.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own published access condition, read September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The largest creative dataset published in this category, 578,750 creatives across 6,015 brands and $1.29 billion of ad spend, reports weekly creative volume and expected hit rate. It reports no cost per asset in any currency.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a creative analytics benchmark in this category, 2026, platform data, period covered not stated by the publisher.</p>

  <p>Six hundred thousand creatives, more than a billion dollars of spend behind them, and not one currency figure per asset. That is not an oversight. It is what the category measures.</p>

  <p>For scale, production is a smaller slice of the budget than most people assume, and shrinking.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production accounted for an average of 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, a survey of 50 global brands across more than ten sectors. It publishes no absolute cost per asset.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about content cost</h2>

  <h3>How much does brand content cost in 2026?</h3>

  <p>There is no single figure, and any page giving you one has not told you what it is pricing. Published bands run from about $0.25 for a bulk image edit to $372,000 committed for a year of a dedicated team. The master table above gives each band against the unit its own source states, with the date and confidence attached.</p>

  <h3>What does a product image cost to produce?</h3>

  <p>Two published studio cards read in September 2026 put standard ecommerce capture at $39 to $50 an image. One adds a $149 studio fee per booking, the other a $300 project minimum. A published Chinese rate card dated July 2026 prices flat lay at 80 to 150 RMB an image on a five-set minimum. Neither card prices sample logistics or reshoots.</p>

  <h3>What does AI video really cost per usable shot?</h3>

  <p>Nobody can tell you honestly, and the gap is the answer. Generation lists at $0.05 to $0.60 a second, so 30 finished seconds costs $1.50 to $18.00 if nothing is retried. At four generations per usable one it is $6 to $72. At the one disclosed ratio of roughly 300 to 1 it is $450 to $5,400. No independent benchmark of that ratio exists.</p>

  <h3>Is a design subscription cheaper than a studio?</h3>

  <p>Wrong question, because they sell different units. A subscription buys capacity with a published cap of one to five items in progress at once, and no card read includes capture, talent booking or rights clearance. A studio card prices a delivered asset. Until you fix the unit, the comparison has no meaning in either direction.</p>

  <h3>Why do content cost benchmarks disagree?</h3>

  <p>Because 28 of 34 results across four buyer phrasings were published by a party selling the work being priced, none by a statistical agency, and not one of the six pages read in full stated how its own figures were collected. Under that, undefined units do the rest: one word, retouch, covers four different services priced more than a hundredfold apart.</p>

  <h3>What does it cost to translate a campaign for China?</h3>

  <p>Published translation rate cards put English to Simplified Chinese at $0.08 to $0.18 a source word, with no method behind the band. Transcreation is priced per hour or per project instead, and no absolute figure for it is published anywhere. The words are also the cheapest part: what it costs to localize a campaign for China covers the visual production the word rate never touches.</p>

  <p>Every band on this page is somebody else's published price, read on a stated date. The only way to find out what your own work costs is to scope it against your own asset list, volumes and markets. <a href="/resources/production-cost">The production cost estimator</a> does that without an email gate, and <a href="/pricing">the pricing page</a> explains how a scope becomes a number.</p>
